# Caddyfile — static origin for mastermind-x.com, behind the TencentEdgeOne CDN
# (migrated from Cloudflare — the CF-specific notes below now mean "the CDN edge").
#
# The CDN terminates public TLS at its edge and connects back to this origin.
# `tls internal` serves Caddy's self-signed cert on :443 so the edge<->origin hop
# is still encrypted; the CDN's origin-fetch/SSL mode must ACCEPT a self-signed
# cert (Cloudflare's "Full", or EdgeOne's equivalent non-strict HTTPS origin pull).
# To upgrade later, replace `tls internal` with a CDN Origin Certificate
# (`tls /path/cert.pem /path/key.pem`) and switch the edge to strict verification.
#
# HTTPS ENFORCEMENT: Force-HTTPS is the EDGE's job — enable "Force HTTPS" + HSTS in
# the EdgeOne console. Cloudflare used to redirect http->https AND auto-upgrade
# stray mixed content; EdgeOne does neither by default, so that toggle is now what
# stops http://mastermind-x.com from serving plaintext. This origin deliberately
# does NOT redirect (an origin redirect behind a CDN can loop the edge's origin
# pull — see disable_redirects below); it only ADDS the HSTS response header on the
# site block, which is safe to emit from behind the CDN and hardens return visits.
# Until the edge toggle is on, :80 serves the SAME site as :443 (mirror block
# below) — never Caddy's implicit empty 200, which the edge can cache for the
# httpS URLs too (the 2026-07-11 /china.html poisoning; scheme-less cache key).
#
# Pre-launch: the whole site is served but marked noindex so it can't be crawled
# before launch. Product login (Supabase) arrives in Slice 2; for a private beta
# right now, front this with Cloudflare Access (email OTP, zero code).
{
	# The CDN edge owns the public http->https redirect for the proxied hosts; don't
	# fight it with Caddy's automatic upgrade, which can loop the edge's origin pull.
	# Only the grey-cloud admin.* host (direct, no CDN) declares its own explicit
	# http:// redirect block below — disable_redirects just suppresses the AUTO ones.
	auto_https disable_redirects
	# Trust Cloudflare's forwarded client IP (so logs show the real visitor).
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}
}

# Shared serving body for mastermind-x.com — imported by BOTH the https site
# block and the plain-HTTP mirror below it, so the two schemes can never drift
# apart. EdgeOne's GET cache key IGNORES the scheme (verified live 2026-07-11
# with a throwaway ?query key): whatever port 80 answers for a path can be
# served from cache to httpS visitors — so :80 must serve the real site with
# the real Cache-Control, never Caddy's implicit empty 200.
(mastermind_static) {
	# Root is /opt/macro/site.served, NOT the git work-tree /opt/macro/site.
	# macro-update rsyncs site/ -> site.served (atomic per-file rename) so a
	# `git reset --hard` refresh can never expose a half-written 0-byte file to be
	# cached by the CDN edge (the 2026-07-03 white-page incident). Paired with the
	# short must-revalidate Cache-Control below as defence-in-depth.
	root * /opt/macro/site.served
	encode zstd gzip

	# Slice 2 backend — FastAPI (macro-api.service) on localhost. Everything under
	# /api/* is proxied here; the rest is served as static files below. Per-user
	# auth lives in the API (Supabase) + (later) a login wall; no shared password.
	#
	# RV W2: the Research Vault's /api/research/* routes (catalog, search, view,
	# download, quota) are ALREADY covered by this /api/* proxy — no extra block is
	# needed. The private research PDFs live ONLY in the private R2 bucket and are
	# fetched server-side by the authed API; they have NO public URL and MUST NOT
	# gain a public static-file rule here (no *.pdf in @public_static below) — the only
	# path to a PDF is the require_user + paid + rate-limited/metered API route.
	# Pre-parse body cap for the ONE public unauthenticated write surface
	# (POST /api/support/ticket, SEE W1). The app also refuses an oversized
	# declared Content-Length, but that check is bypassable with chunked
	# transfer-encoding — the app never sees a length to refuse. Only the edge can
	# enforce a real cap, because only the edge sits before the body is read.
	#
	# Scoped to /api/support/* on purpose, NOT the whole /api/* tree: a global cap
	# here would also apply to the Stripe webhook (invoice objects with long line-item
	# lists) and the /api/collect analytics batch (up to 40 events), and truncating
	# either to fix a contact form would be a bad trade.
	request_body /api/support/* {
		max_size 64KB
	}

	reverse_proxy /api/* 127.0.0.1:8000 {
		# TRUSTED peer identity for per-client rate limiting. mastermind-x.com is
		# behind EdgeOne, so every real-client header the app reads (EO-Client-IP,
		# CF-Connecting-IP, X-Forwarded-For, …) is ATTACKER-SUPPLIABLE at the origin:
		# a bot that rotates EO-Client-IP per request lands every hit in a fresh
		# rate-limit bucket. header_up REPLACES any inbound X-MM-Peer with the real
		# TCP peer, so this one header cannot be spoofed (same mechanic as
		# X-Admin-Client-IP on the admin host below). app/support.py rate-limits on
		# BOTH keys — the claimed IP tightly, the trusted peer loosely (CN traffic
		# legitimately aggregates behind a handful of EdgeOne edge IPs).
		#
		# The eo-*/cf-* headers are deliberately NOT scrubbed: EdgeOne sets them for
		# real in CN routing and other consumers (analytics geo, the site gate) read
		# them. This adds a trustworthy signal rather than removing untrusted ones.
		header_up X-MM-Peer {remote_host}
	}

	# Live Tape websocket — same-origin relay to macro-api (app/tape.py).
	# China-reachable (same-origin, like the /sb Supabase proxy). Placed with
	# the /api/* proxy so a ws upgrade never reaches the HTML gate matchers.
	reverse_proxy /ws/tape 127.0.0.1:8000

	# VPS live plane — artifacts are owned by systemd lanes and published by
	# atomic rename outside both the git work-tree and site.served. This prevents
	# a main-branch pull/rsync from rolling a fresh tick back to the last commit.
	#
	# SECURITY: quotes, breadth, and official release lifecycle are explicitly
	# reviewed PUBLIC assets. Every other /live/* artifact and the heatmap remain
	# inside @reg_asset below and therefore pass registration + paywall checks
	# before the external file is considered. Never add a top-level /live/*
	# file_server: that would bypass the default-deny static boundary.
	@vps_public_live {
		path /live/quotes.json /live/breadth.json /live/release_publications.json
		file {
			root /var/lib/macro-live/public
		}
	}
	handle @vps_public_live {
		root * /var/lib/macro-live/public
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		file_server
	}
	# Zero-downtime fallback before live-setup creates the external quote.
	handle /live/quotes.json {
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}
	handle /live/breadth.json {
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}
	handle /live/release_publications.json {
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		file_server
	}

	# Protected external artifacts are served only from inside @reg_asset's
	# authenticated route. The matcher also provides a zero-downtime fallback:
	# when a file is absent externally, the canonical site.served file wins.
	@vps_external {
		path /live/* /marketdata/sp500_heatmap.json
		file {
			root /var/lib/macro-live/public
		}
	}

	# ── Bare-path aliases (backlink + crawler hygiene) ────────────────────────
	#
	# Extensionless URL guesses — /blog, /start, /plans — carry no ".html" and no
	# trailing slash, so they miss BOTH the *.html document matcher and the
	# /blog/* style prefix exclusions below. They therefore landed in @reg_asset
	# and died at the registration wall's 401 JSON: a crawler or an inbound link
	# got a hard auth error where a page exists. These 301 them onto the canonical
	# sitemap form instead.
	#
	# `redir` sorts BEFORE `handle`/`file_server` in Caddy's default directive
	# order, so these resolve ahead of the Site Access Boundary below (this snippet
	# is imported at the top level of the site block — it is NOT wrapped in an
	# explicit `route`, which would swap ordering to written order). Redirecting
	# onto a regwalled .html target is intentional and safe: the gate still runs on
	# the redirected request.
	#
	# Section hubs -> the canonical /<section>/index.html the sitemap lists.
	redir /blog /blog/index.html 301
	redir /blog/ /blog/index.html 301
	redir /learn /learn/index.html 301
	redir /learn/ /learn/index.html 301
	redir /products /products/index.html 301
	redir /products/ /products/index.html 301
	redir /tools /tools/index.html 301
	redir /tools/ /tools/index.html 301
	redir /stocks /stocks/index.html 301
	redir /stocks/ /stocks/index.html 301
	redir /research /research/index.html 301
	redir /research/ /research/index.html 301
	# Top-level pages -> the same name with its extension.
	redir /start /start.html 301
	redir /plans /plans.html 301
	redir /macro /macro.html 301
	redir /about /about.html 301
	redir /support /support.html 301
	redir /privacy /privacy.html 301
	redir /terms /terms.html 301
	redir /disclaimer /disclaimer.html 301
	redir /us_stocks /us_stocks.html 301
	redir /research_vault /research_vault.html 301
	redir /confluence_screener /confluence_screener.html 301

	# ── Site Access Boundary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
	#
	# DEFAULT-DENY STATIC SERVING. Only the explicit public funnel/assets listed
	# in config/site_access.yml bypass authentication. Every other file — HTML,
	# JSON, data-bearing JS, images, generated model outputs, and future unknown
	# extensions — passes two independent fail-closed checks:
	#   1. /api/regwall/check  — valid Supabase account.
	#   2. /api/paywall/check  — when PAYWALL_ENABLED=1, active/trialing
	#                            entitlement carrying feature `site_full`.
	#
	# Documents also pass the pre-existing fail-open IP/country gate FIRST. The
	# public landing/plans funnel retains the one-page-still-up behavior. Product
	# paths never fail open: if either auth service is unavailable, Caddy returns
	# a lock/503 response and NEVER falls through to file_server.
	#
	# Keep PUBLIC-BOUNDARY below byte-aligned with config/site_access.yml. The
	# hermetic boundary test rejects drift.

	# Internal previews/QA and proprietary research tools are not customer
	# content, even for entitled accounts. The seven research tools are available
	# only through admin.mastermind-x.com's authenticated /research-tools/ route.
	@never_site {
		path /_mockup_ccw_credit_desk.html /_mockup_research_vault.html /qa_bottom_sensors.html /status.html /committee.html /measurement.html /crossasset.html /signal_lab.html /tech_lab.html /macro_signals.html /factors.html
	}
	handle @never_site {
		header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
		header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
		respond "Not found" 404
	}

	# Protected HTML navigations: IP gate -> registration -> paid feature gate.
	@reg_html {
		path *.html
		not path /api/* /ws/tape /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /stock_seasonality.html /about.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/* /_mockup_ccw_credit_desk.html /_mockup_research_vault.html /qa_bottom_sensors.html /status.html
	}

	handle @reg_html {
		route {
			# Stage 1 — IP/country gate (deliberately fail-open).
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/gate/check
				@allow status 2xx
				handle_response @allow {
				}
			}
			# Stage 2 — registration wall (fail-closed).
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/regwall/check
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				header_up X-Original-Kind document
				@rallow status 2xx
				handle_response @rallow {
				}
			}
			# Stage 3 — paid entitlement wall (disabled until launch prerequisites
			# pass; the endpoint still exists and fails closed if miswired).
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/paywall/check
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				header_up X-Original-Kind document
				@pallow status 2xx
				handle_response @pallow {
				}
			}
			header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
			header Vary "Cookie"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	# Protected non-HTML and extensionless paths. This is the closure of the
	# original leak: direct JSON/JS/model-output URLs no longer bypass auth.
	# PUBLIC-BOUNDARY-START
	@reg_asset {
		not path /api/* /ws/tape / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /research_vault_app.js /about.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /favicon.svg /favicon.ico /apple-touch-icon.png /robots.txt /88bb90b05303e3cf469878ebc4dc7543.txt /sitemap.xml /llms.txt /brand-facts.json /onboard.css /onboard.js /tier_preview.css /tier_preview.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /navigation-refresh.css /product-nav-icons.css /logo_config.js /stock-logos.js /theme.js /dashboard-icons.css /dashboard-icons.js /chart_i18n.js /timemachine.js /tablesort.js /charts.js /risk_state_live.js /release_publications_live.js /heatmap.js /stocktable.js /globe-deck.js /sky.js /hub-welcome.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /world-110m.json /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /live/quotes.json /live/breadth.json /live/release_publications.json /prophet/showcase.json /seasonalitydata/methodology.json /seasonalitydata/index.json /seasonalitydata/entities/SPY.json /factordata/tech_lab.json /stock_seasonality.css /stock_seasonality.html /stock_seasonality.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /factordata/tech_events/* /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/* /fonts/* *.html
	}
	# PUBLIC-BOUNDARY-END
	handle @reg_asset {
		route {
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/regwall/check
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				header_up X-Original-Kind asset
				@rallow status 2xx
				handle_response @rallow {
				}
			}
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/paywall/check
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				header_up X-Original-Kind asset
				@pallow status 2xx
				handle_response @pallow {
				}
			}
			header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
			header Vary "Cookie"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			handle @vps_external {
				root * /var/lib/macro-live/public
				file_server
			}
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	@gate_html {
		# Public funnel only — IP gate retains its fail-open availability contract.
		path / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /stock_seasonality.html /about.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/*
	}

	handle @gate_html {
		route {
			# Ask the gate about THIS request. `method GET` + `rewrite` turn the
			# sub-request into GET /api/gate/check while preserving the visitor's
			# headers (EO-Client-IPCountry / EO-Connecting-IP / CF-*) so the gate
			# can read them.
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/gate/check

				# 2xx (204 allow): empty handle_response → CONTINUE to file_server.
				@allow status 2xx
				handle_response @allow {
				}
				# Non-2xx (403 block): the default copy_response streams the gate's
				# body (the self-contained coming-soon page) + its no-store/X-Gate
				# headers back to the visitor and STOPS — so the max-age header
				# below never touches the block response (it keeps no-store).
			}
			# Reached ONLY on allow — serve the file with the normal short HTML TTL.
			header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	handle_errors {
		# Protected documents fail CLOSED on gate-upstream failure.
		@reg_html_err {
			path *.html
			not path /api/* /ws/tape /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /stock_seasonality.html /about.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/* /_mockup_ccw_credit_desk.html /_mockup_research_vault.html /qa_bottom_sensors.html /status.html
		}
		handle @reg_html_err {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			redir * /?signin=1 302
		}

		# Protected assets fail CLOSED too. The JSON response is intentionally
		# small and non-cacheable; never substitute the requested static file.
		#
		# /live/wires.json and /live/intelligence.json are listed below for a
		# DIFFERENT reason than the public entries beside them: they stay
		# registration-gated in @reg_asset above, but a MISSING file must reach the
		# client as a real 404. Without the exclusion, file_server's 404 enters
		# handle_errors and is rewritten to the 503 "temporarily unavailable" body
		# — so before either daemon artifact exists, every registered visitor would
		# be told the desk is broken instead of the module simply staying hidden.
		# Excluding them here cannot leak a file: the error path has no file_server
		# (asserted by
		# tests/test_regwall_json_gate.py), so content is still only ever served
		# from inside `handle @reg_asset` after the gate returns 2xx.
		@reg_asset_err {
			not path /api/* /ws/tape / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /research_vault_app.js /about.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /favicon.svg /favicon.ico /apple-touch-icon.png /robots.txt /88bb90b05303e3cf469878ebc4dc7543.txt /sitemap.xml /llms.txt /brand-facts.json /onboard.css /onboard.js /tier_preview.css /tier_preview.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /navigation-refresh.css /product-nav-icons.css /logo_config.js /stock-logos.js /theme.js /dashboard-icons.css /dashboard-icons.js /chart_i18n.js /timemachine.js /tablesort.js /charts.js /risk_state_live.js /release_publications_live.js /heatmap.js /stocktable.js /globe-deck.js /sky.js /hub-welcome.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /world-110m.json /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /live/quotes.json /live/breadth.json /live/release_publications.json /live/wires.json /live/intelligence.json /prophet/showcase.json /seasonalitydata/methodology.json /seasonalitydata/index.json /seasonalitydata/entities/SPY.json /factordata/tech_lab.json /stock_seasonality.css /stock_seasonality.html /stock_seasonality.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /factordata/tech_events/* /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/* /fonts/* *.html
		}
		handle @reg_asset_err {
			header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
			header Content-Type "application/json"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			respond `{"error":"site_access_temporarily_unavailable"}` 503
		}

		# Public funnel only: retain fail-open availability if the IP gate is down.
		@gate_html_err {
			path / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /stock_seasonality.html /about.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/*
		}
		handle @gate_html_err {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			file_server {
				status 200
				index index.html
			}
		}

	}

	# Only explicit public assets can reach this direct file server.
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}

	# --- CDN cache safety (2026-07-03 white-page incident) ----------------------
	# A response with NO Cache-Control gets EdgeOne's LONG default TTL, so any
	# transient bad body would be STUCK at the edge for hours. A short,
	# must-revalidate TTL caps any bad edge entry (HTML <=60s, other static <=300s)
	# and forces the edge to revalidate against the (healthy) origin. Mirrors the
	# greydeercapital.com block below. The zero-window structural fix (atomic
	# swap + flock + 0-byte rsync guard) lives in update.sh.
	#
	# NOTE: HTML's max-age=60 is now set INSIDE the Site Access Gate handle above
	# (on the allow path only), so a blocked visitor's coming-soon page keeps its
	# Cache-Control: no-store instead of being clobbered to a cacheable 60s. Do not
	# re-add a blanket `header @html Cache-Control` here — it would re-cache blocks.
	#
	# Only reviewed PUBLIC assets receive shared-cache headers. A blanket
	# extension matcher here would override a protected response's no-store and
	# turn the CDN into an authentication bypass.
	@public_static {
		path /favicon.svg /favicon.ico /apple-touch-icon.png /robots.txt /88bb90b05303e3cf469878ebc4dc7543.txt /sitemap.xml /llms.txt /brand-facts.json /onboard.css /onboard.js /tier_preview.css /tier_preview.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /product-nav-icons.css /theme.js /dashboard-icons.css /dashboard-icons.js /chart_i18n.js /timemachine.js /tablesort.js /charts.js /risk_state_live.js /release_publications_live.js /heatmap.js /stocktable.js /globe-deck.js /sky.js /hub-welcome.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /world-110m.json /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /research_vault_app.js /prophet/showcase.json /seasonalitydata/methodology.json /seasonalitydata/index.json /seasonalitydata/entities/SPY.json /stock_seasonality.css /stock_seasonality.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /fonts/*
		not query v=*
	}
	header @public_static Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	# Temporary Terminal ingest carve-outs. They are explicit public policy
	# entries and can be removed when that separate flow authenticates.
	@factordata_public {
		path /factordata/tech_lab.json /factordata/tech_events/*
	}
	header @factordata_public Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	# Reviewed public assets carrying ?v=<hash> are safe to cache forever: the
	# hash is derived from the file body, so a change yields a NEW URL and a stale
	# body cannot be stuck at the edge under a reused name.
	# scripts/optimize_assets.py stamps ?v= on every local .js/.css post-render, so
	# the shared bundle (theme.js/heatmap.js/theme.css, ~143KB) stops being
	# re-revalidated on every page navigation — the dominant mobile cost. Refs
	# WITHOUT ?v= (e.g. a page rendered before the sweep) keep the short TTL above.
	@public_versioned {
		path /onboard.css /onboard.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /product-nav-icons.css /theme.js /release_publications_live.js /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/*
		query v=*
	}
	header @public_versioned Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	header {
		# PUBLIC (was pre-launch noindex): the site is now open to search engines.
		# The old `X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"` header was removed
		# here so crawlers can index it (paired with robots.txt + sitemap.xml in
		# site/). Re-add that header to pull the whole site back out of search.
		# Basic hardening.
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies "none"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), usb=()"
		# Minimal enforceable CSP that does not break the legacy inline-script
		# pages. Tightening script-src/style-src needs a nonce migration.
		Content-Security-Policy "base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
		-Server
	}
}

www.mastermind-x.com {
	import mastermind_static

	header {
		# Enforce HTTPS on return visits (the edge should set this too). 1 year,
		# matching admin.*; no includeSubDomains — other subdomains manage their own.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	}

	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with the CDN's non-strict HTTPS origin pull.
	tls internal

	# Access logs go to journald (`journalctl -u caddy`). We deliberately do NOT
	# write a file log: the stock caddy.service systemd sandbox only permits writes
	# under /var/lib/caddy, so a /var/log/caddy file sink fails with EACCES and
	# crashes startup. journald is the idiomatic, sandbox-safe sink.
}

# ---- apex mastermind-x.com - DIRECT redirect to the CDN-fronted www --------
# The apex A record points straight at this origin (it CANNOT be a CNAME to the
# EdgeOne edge, because mastermind-x.com also carries the MX for email; a CNAME
# at the apex illegally shadows MX and makes mail flap). So the apex is a direct
# host here: Caddy issues a REAL public Let's Encrypt cert (ACME HTTP-01 on :80
# works because apex A -> this box) and 301s everything to https://www, which
# stays behind EdgeOne. Kept OUT of the www serving block above so EdgeOne's www
# origin-pull (Host: www.mastermind-x.com) can never land on this redirect+loop.
http://mastermind-x.com {
	redir https://www.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

mastermind-x.com {
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	redir https://www.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

# ---- plain-HTTP (:80) mirror — the 2026-07-11 /china.html poisoning fix ------
# With `auto_https disable_redirects` and https-only site blocks, Caddy answered
# ANY plain-HTTP request for these hosts with its IMPLICIT catch-all: an empty
# 200 with no body and no Cache-Control. EdgeOne's origin pull follows the
# visitor's scheme (Force-HTTPS off at the edge), so one bot GET of
# http://mastermind-x.com/china.html primed the edge with that empty 200 —
# and because the edge cache key ignores the scheme, httpS visitors got the
# dead page for hours (no Cache-Control -> EdgeOne's long default TTL).
# Serving the REAL site on :80 means an http origin pull can only ever cache a
# real page bounded by max-age=60. Deliberately NOT a redirect: if the edge's
# origin-pull were ever http-only, an origin http->https redirect would loop
# (see the header notes above) — the edge console owns Force-HTTPS.
http://www.mastermind-x.com {
	import mastermind_static
}

# ---- plain-HTTP catch-all — kill Caddy's implicit empty 200 -------------------
# Every OTHER hostname that reaches this box over :80 (app./bot./greydeer/
# slate/maltese/stray Host headers) used to fall into the same implicit empty
# 200 — the exact cacheable-poison shape above, just waiting for a CDN in front
# of any of those hosts. A 421 with no-store is never cacheable, so the port-80
# path can't poison anything again. Explicit http:// blocks (the mastermind
# mirror above, the admin redirect below) outrank this hostless block, and
# Caddy answers ACME HTTP-01 challenges before routing, so admin.* cert
# issuance is unaffected.
http:// {
	header Cache-Control "no-store"
	respond "Misdirected: use https://" 421
}

# ---- app.mastermind-x.com — the Mastermind Terminal (Next.js) ----------------
# The charting SaaS frontend, served by terminal.service (next start) on :3000.
# Needs a Cloudflare DNS A record: app -> 146.190.142.17 (proxied), SSL mode "Full".
app.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# --- Terminal /data static serving (2026-07-03) ------------------------------
	# Serve the terminal's static data JSONs DIRECTLY from disk at Caddy, bypassing
	# Next.js: `next start` 404s public/ files added AFTER the build (verified live:
	# a rsync'd NEW <SYM>.fund.json 404'd until rebuild), and the fundamentals
	# pipeline rsyncs NEW per-symbol files daily (fund/opts/tx waves). Caddy's
	# file_server has no build manifest — a new file serves on the next request.
	# Cache headers set here (plain header: file_server emits no Cache-Control, so
	# no defer needed). EdgeOne still RefreshHits until an owner console rule exists.
	handle /data/* {
		root * /opt/terminal/terminal/public
		@manifest path /data/manifest.json
		@rest {
			path /data/*
			not path /data/manifest.json
		}
		header @manifest Cache-Control "public, s-maxage=60, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
		header @rest Cache-Control "public, s-maxage=300, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		-Server
	}
	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with Cloudflare SSL mode = "Full".
	tls internal
}

# ---- bot.mastermind-x.com — Brain BOT serve-only mirror (uvicorn :8001) -------
# The Mastermind Brain dashboard (paper-trading FastAPI), served by
# mastermind.service on 127.0.0.1:8001. The app handles its own auth
# (MASTERMIND_PASSWORD in /opt/mastermind/.env; currently disabled). MUST live
# here — without a site block, Caddy's `tls internal` has no cert for this SNI and
# the Cloudflare<->origin handshake fails with HTTP 525. DNS A record:
# bot -> 146.190.142.17 (proxied), SSL mode "Full".
bot.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		-Server
	}
	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with Cloudflare SSL mode = "Full".
	tls internal
}

# ---- admin.mastermind-x.com — the Admin console (python -m admin :8787) -------
# Authenticated control + observability console (admin.service on 127.0.0.1:8787).
# UNLIKE the other hosts this is GREY-CLOUD / DNS-only (A record admin ->
# 146.190.142.17, NOT proxied through Cloudflare), so Caddy provisions a REAL
# Let's Encrypt cert over HTTP-01 (port 80 is kept open to all in ufw by
# firewall-cloudflare.sh's grey-cloud escape hatch) — no `tls internal`, no
# Cloudflare in front. The app enforces password + signed-session auth
# (ADMIN_PASSWORD in /etc/macro-admin.env); Caddy adds anti-framing + HSTS headers.
#
# The global `auto_https disable_redirects` (correct for the CF-fronted hosts)
# would otherwise serve this host over PLAINTEXT http:// with no upgrade — and the
# login posts the shared password. So we add an explicit http:// block that
# force-redirects to https:// (Caddy still answers the ACME HTTP-01 challenge on
# :80 ahead of this redirect, so cert issuance/renewal is unaffected).
http://admin.mastermind-x.com {
	redir https://admin.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

admin.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Reuse the generated research pages, but only behind the admin session. The
	# prefix is stripped before file lookup so every existing relative CSS, JS,
	# and data dependency continues to resolve without a second page build.
	handle_path /research-tools/* {
		forward_auth 127.0.0.1:8787 {
			uri /api/auth-check
			header_up X-Admin-Client-IP {remote_host}
		}
		root * /opt/macro/site.served
		header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
		file_server
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787 {
			# admin.* is grey-cloud / direct-to-origin (no CDN), so the TCP peer IS
			# the real client. Inject it as a trusted header the app reads for its
			# per-client login lockout; replacing a caller-supplied value prevents
			# brute-force clients from rotating lockout buckets.
			header_up X-Admin-Client-IP {remote_host}
		}
	}
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		-Server
	}
}

# ---- press properties (D14 W1.5) — mastermindx.ai + blog.mastermind-x.com ----
# COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE. This block is the SERVING half of the press cutover
# and it is inert until the day the properties go live.
#
# THE PAIRING (CI-enforced, tests/test_press_properties.py::the cutover sync
# guard): the commit that uncomments the block between the two marker lines
# below MUST be the same commit that sets `cutover: true` in config/press.yml,
# and vice versa. The test asserts BOTH directions, so forgetting either half
# turns the PR red rather than shipping a live vhost with no content routed to
# it (or a routed emit with nothing serving it).
#
# UNCOMMENT MECHANICALLY, do not hand-edit. The exact one-line sed lives in
# app/deploy/README.md § "Press properties (W1.5) — cutover runbook"; it is
# deliberately NOT quoted here, because a sed range written out in this comment
# is itself matched by that range and gets uncommented into a syntax error
# (found exactly that way, by validating the result). Every line between the
# markers starts with '#' at COLUMN 0 — the leading tabs live AFTER the '#' —
# so stripping one leading '#' from each interior line yields the intended
# config verbatim.
#
# DNS FIRST, THEN THIS. Order matters and it is not reversible in the other
# direction: point the A records at 146.190.142.17 (Spaceship DNS: mastermindx.ai
# @ and www; blog.mastermind-x.com), confirm with `dig`, THEN merge the cutover
# PR. Uncommenting before DNS resolves here is not dangerous — Caddy would simply
# retry ACME HTTP-01 until the records appear, and no visitor can reach a host
# that does not point at the box — but the pairing test forbids it anyway,
# because the config half without the DNS half is a claim nobody verified.
#
# REAL CERTIFICATES, NOT `tls internal`. Every other host in this file sits
# behind the EdgeOne CDN and uses a self-signed origin cert. These two do not:
# they are GREY-CLOUD / direct A records, exactly like admin.mastermind-x.com
# above, so Caddy provisions genuine Let's Encrypt certs over HTTP-01 on :80
# (ufw keeps :80 open for the grey-cloud escape hatch). A `tls internal` here
# would serve a self-signed cert straight to a browser — the test asserts its
# absence.
#
# WHY THE http:// BLOCKS EXIST: the global `auto_https disable_redirects` (right
# for the CDN-fronted hosts, which must not fight the edge's own redirect) means
# Caddy adds NO automatic http->https upgrade. Without an explicit redirect these
# hosts would answer plain http from the port-80 catch-all as a 421. Caddy answers
# ACME HTTP-01 challenges BEFORE routing, so these redirects never interfere with
# issuance or renewal — same arrangement admin.* has run on for months.
#
# ONE CANONICAL HOST. The apex (mastermindx.ai) serves; www 301s to it. Serving
# BOTH names off one site block would put every page on two live URLs and rely
# entirely on rel=canonical to sort it out — a hint, not an instruction, and one
# that costs a crawl of every duplicate to even read. config/press.yml
# `base_url` is https://mastermindx.ai, so the redirect target is the same host
# the sitemap, the RSS feed and every JSON-LD `url` already name. www gets its
# own site block and therefore its own ACME certificate (a redirect still needs
# a valid cert to perform the redirect over https).
#
# HSTS STARTS SHORT ON PURPOSE: max-age=300, not a year. HSTS is the one header
# a browser will not let you take back — once a client has cached a long
# max-age it REFUSES plain http for that host for the full duration, so a
# certificate or DNS mistake on a brand-new domain is unreachable-by-design
# until the header expires. Five minutes is enough to be real and short enough
# to be survivable. Raise it to 31536000 after a clean week (runbook step 5).
#
# ROLLBACK: revert the cutover PR. That re-comments this block and puts
# `cutover: false` back in the same move, which is the point of pairing them.
# The DNS records can stay pointed here — with the block commented, those hosts
# fall through to the :80 catch-all / no TLS host and serve nothing.
#
# The served roots are published by app/deploy/update.sh, which rsyncs
# properties/news -> press_news.served and properties/research ->
# press_research.served with the same atomic-rename + --min-size=1 protections
# site.served uses (the 2026-07-03 white-page incident).
# PRESS-CUTOVER-BEGIN
#mastermindx.ai {
#	root * /opt/macro/press_news.served
#	encode zstd gzip
#	file_server {
#		index index.html
#	}
#	handle_errors {
